  "prompt_text": "The figure presents a schematic design for a lithium battery that uses a halogen-containing solid electrolyte to bridge the mismatch between a Li metal anode and a high-voltage cathode. At the center is a simplified cell cross-section, with a thin interfacial region next to the lithium metal and a particulate cathode region on the opposite side. Colored spheres represent different halide ions—F, Cl, Br, and I—suggesting that the electrolyte composition can be tuned with different halogen chemistries.\n\nThe main idea is that different parts of the cell favor different electrolyte formulations. Near the Li metal anode, the electrolyte appears designed as a buffer or protective layer, labeled with concepts such as mixed-metal or dual-halide solid electrolytes and a general Li–M–X type. Toward the high-voltage cathode, the electrolyte is shown as a cathode-facing coating or composite cathode component, described as halogen-rich and also dual-halide, with LPSX-type material noted underneath. The central “halogen-containing SE” label ties these regions together, implying a family of solid electrolytes engineered for both interfaces. Overall, the figure emphasizes interfacial stabilization through compositionally tailored halide-based solid electrolytes across the full battery.",
